1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,739
Most people imagine an icebreaker smashing through

2
00:00:03,739 --> 00:00:05,085
ice like a hammer,

3
00:00:05,085 --> 00:00:07,105
or cutting it like a knife.

4
00:00:07,285 --> 00:00:08,376
It does neither.

5
00:00:08,556 --> 00:00:11,516
An icebreaker drives up onto the ice,

6
00:00:11,516 --> 00:00:14,076
and then crushes it with its own

7
00:00:14,076 --> 00:00:15,995
weight, again and again,

8
00:00:15,995 --> 00:00:17,995
thousands of times a day.

9
00:00:18,175 --> 00:00:21,687
Today, ten parts of an icebreaker,

10
00:00:21,687 --> 00:00:24,166
and why each one exists.

11
00:00:24,516 --> 00:00:26,548
Sea ice can be more than a

12
00:00:26,548 --> 00:00:28,893
metre thick, and it can trap a

13
00:00:28,893 --> 00:00:30,612
normal ship for weeks,

14
00:00:30,612 --> 00:00:32,410
or even crush its hull.

15
00:00:32,590 --> 00:00:35,227
In places like the Arctic and the

16
00:00:35,227 --> 00:00:38,982
Baltic Sea, icebreakers keep ports and shipping

17
00:00:38,982 --> 00:00:41,379
lanes open through the winter.

18
00:00:41,559 --> 00:00:44,073
They are some of the toughest ships

19
00:00:44,073 --> 00:00:46,659
ever built, and almost every part of

20
00:00:46,659 --> 00:00:48,455
them is designed for ice.

21
00:00:48,806 --> 00:00:51,868
As always, every fact here comes from

22
00:00:51,868 --> 00:00:53,109
a named source,

23
00:00:53,109 --> 00:00:54,434
ice class rules,

24
00:00:54,434 --> 00:00:57,248
shipbuilders and naval references,

25
00:00:57,248 --> 00:00:59,731
all listed in the description.

26
00:00:59,911 --> 00:01:01,327
Let's start with the bow.

27
00:01:01,677 --> 00:01:04,643
Why does an icebreaker have a long,

28
00:01:04,643 --> 00:01:06,762
sloped bow, like a spoon?

29
00:01:06,942 --> 00:01:09,220
So it can climb on top of

30
00:01:09,220 --> 00:01:09,949
the ice.

31
00:01:10,129 --> 00:01:12,453
A normal ship's bow is steep,

32
00:01:12,453 --> 00:01:14,937
and it would hit thick ice head

33
00:01:14,937 --> 00:01:15,899
on and stop.

34
00:01:16,079 --> 00:01:19,331
An icebreaker's bow is shallow and angled,

35
00:01:19,331 --> 00:01:21,267
so when it meets the ice,

36
00:01:21,267 --> 00:01:23,358
the ship slides up onto it.

37
00:01:23,709 --> 00:01:25,411
Once the bow is on top,

38
00:01:25,411 --> 00:01:28,076
the weight of thousands of tonnes of

39
00:01:28,076 --> 00:01:29,409
ship presses down.

40
00:01:29,589 --> 00:01:31,788
Ice is strong when you push it

41
00:01:31,788 --> 00:01:32,814
from the side,

42
00:01:32,814 --> 00:01:35,012
but weak when you bend it from

43
00:01:35,012 --> 00:01:35,452
above.

44
00:01:35,632 --> 00:01:38,222
The ice sheet cracks under the weight,

45
00:01:38,222 --> 00:01:40,745
and the broken pieces are pushed down

46
00:01:40,745 --> 00:01:42,517
and aside, under the hull.

47
00:01:42,867 --> 00:01:44,988
If the ice is too thick to

48
00:01:44,988 --> 00:01:46,294
break in one go,

49
00:01:46,294 --> 00:01:49,231
the icebreaker backs up and rams it,

50
00:01:49,231 --> 00:01:51,597
sliding further up each time.

51
00:01:51,777 --> 00:01:53,335
This is called ramming,

52
00:01:53,335 --> 00:01:55,502
and it can take many attempts to

53
00:01:55,502 --> 00:01:57,602
get through a ridge of piled up

54
00:01:57,602 --> 00:01:57,873
ice.

55
00:01:58,053 --> 00:01:59,039
It is slow,

56
00:01:59,039 --> 00:02:01,102
noisy and violent work,

57
00:02:01,102 --> 00:02:03,703
but it opens a path for other

58
00:02:03,703 --> 00:02:04,241
ships.

59
00:02:04,591 --> 00:02:06,745
Why is the hull of an icebreaker

60
00:02:06,745 --> 00:02:08,968
much thicker in a band around the

61
00:02:08,968 --> 00:02:09,641
waterline?

62
00:02:09,821 --> 00:02:12,294
That band is called the ice belt.

63
00:02:12,474 --> 00:02:14,371
It is the part of the hull

64
00:02:14,371 --> 00:02:15,685
that hits the ice,

65
00:02:15,685 --> 00:02:18,385
scrapes along it and gets squeezed by

66
00:02:18,385 --> 00:02:18,604
it.

67
00:02:18,784 --> 00:02:20,226
On a large icebreaker,

68
00:02:20,226 --> 00:02:22,980
the steel there can be several centimetres

69
00:02:22,980 --> 00:02:25,341
thick, much thicker than on a normal

70
00:02:25,341 --> 00:02:25,669
ship.

71
00:02:26,019 --> 00:02:29,366
Inside, the ice belt is also supported

72
00:02:29,366 --> 00:02:32,713
by extra frames placed close together,

73
00:02:32,713 --> 00:02:35,620
like the ribs of a strong animal.

74
00:02:35,800 --> 00:02:37,658
Ice does not push evenly.

75
00:02:37,838 --> 00:02:39,150
It hits in sharp,

76
00:02:39,150 --> 00:02:42,934
concentrated points, which can dent normal steel.

77
00:02:43,114 --> 00:02:46,023
The thick plates and dense frames spread

78
00:02:46,023 --> 00:02:47,259
those forces out.

79
00:02:47,610 --> 00:02:50,129
The steel itself is special.

80
00:02:50,309 --> 00:02:54,205
Normal steel becomes brittle in extreme cold,

81
00:02:54,205 --> 00:02:56,369
and can crack like glass.

82
00:02:56,549 --> 00:03:00,005
Icebreakers use steel that stays tough at

83
00:03:00,005 --> 00:03:01,860
very low temperatures,

84
00:03:01,860 --> 00:03:04,978
often far below minus thirty degrees.

85
00:03:05,158 --> 00:03:06,241
In the Arctic,

86
00:03:06,241 --> 00:03:08,484
even the choice of metal is a

87
00:03:08,484 --> 00:03:09,953
matter of survival.

88
00:03:10,303 --> 00:03:13,026
Why are icebreaker hulls rounded at the

89
00:03:13,026 --> 00:03:14,912
sides, instead of straight?

90
00:03:15,092 --> 00:03:17,404
So that ice pushes the ship up,

91
00:03:17,404 --> 00:03:19,120
instead of crushing it.

92
00:03:19,300 --> 00:03:22,561
When floating ice is pressed together by

93
00:03:22,561 --> 00:03:24,028
wind and currents,

94
00:03:24,028 --> 00:03:26,065
it can close in on a ship

95
00:03:26,065 --> 00:03:27,369
from both sides.

96
00:03:27,549 --> 00:03:30,104
A straight sided hull takes the full

97
00:03:30,104 --> 00:03:32,658
squeeze, like a nut in a nutcracker.

98
00:03:33,008 --> 00:03:36,661
A rounded hull turns that sideways squeeze

99
00:03:36,661 --> 00:03:39,010
partly into an upward push.

100
00:03:39,190 --> 00:03:41,834
The ice slides down along the curve

101
00:03:41,834 --> 00:03:43,269
and lifts the ship,

102
00:03:43,269 --> 00:03:45,007
instead of crushing it.

103
00:03:45,187 --> 00:03:47,327
The famous polar ship Fram,

104
00:03:47,327 --> 00:03:50,578
built for the explorer Fridtjof Nansen in

105
00:03:50,578 --> 00:03:52,322
the eighteen nineties,

106
00:03:52,322 --> 00:03:53,511
used this idea.

107
00:03:53,691 --> 00:03:56,232
It was frozen into the Arctic ice

108
00:03:56,232 --> 00:03:58,079
on purpose and survived.

109
00:03:58,430 --> 00:04:00,635
Many ships trapped in ice in the

110
00:04:00,635 --> 00:04:02,428
past, with straight sides,

111
00:04:02,428 --> 00:04:03,944
were crushed and sank.

112
00:04:04,124 --> 00:04:06,987
The rounded shape makes the ship roll

113
00:04:06,987 --> 00:04:08,456
more in open water,

114
00:04:08,456 --> 00:04:11,241
which is uncomfortable for the crew.

115
00:04:11,421 --> 00:04:12,580
But in the ice,

116
00:04:12,580 --> 00:04:15,670
comfort is less important than survival.

117
00:04:16,021 --> 00:04:19,248
Why does an icebreaker rock itself from

118
00:04:19,248 --> 00:04:21,233
side to side on purpose?

119
00:04:21,413 --> 00:04:23,538
To avoid getting stuck.

120
00:04:23,718 --> 00:04:25,605
Ice can grip the sides of a

121
00:04:25,605 --> 00:04:27,282
ship with huge friction.

122
00:04:27,462 --> 00:04:29,955
Large tanks on both sides of the

123
00:04:29,955 --> 00:04:32,838
hull pump water quickly from one side

124
00:04:32,838 --> 00:04:33,850
to the other,

125
00:04:33,850 --> 00:04:36,499
making the ship roll a few degrees

126
00:04:36,499 --> 00:04:37,668
back and forth.

127
00:04:38,018 --> 00:04:40,612
That rolling motion breaks the grip of

128
00:04:40,612 --> 00:04:41,978
the ice on the hull,

129
00:04:41,978 --> 00:04:44,027
like wiggling a stuck key in a

130
00:04:44,027 --> 00:04:44,368
lock.

131
00:04:44,548 --> 00:04:46,677
It also helps the ship turn in

132
00:04:46,677 --> 00:04:48,947
thick ice and break free when it

133
00:04:48,947 --> 00:04:49,657
is pinned.

134
00:04:49,837 --> 00:04:53,216
Some icebreakers can also pump water forward

135
00:04:53,216 --> 00:04:55,596
and backward to tilt the bow up

136
00:04:55,596 --> 00:04:56,211
or down.

137
00:04:56,561 --> 00:04:58,987
The heeling system is powerful.

138
00:04:59,167 --> 00:05:01,168
Hundreds of tonnes of water can be

139
00:05:01,168 --> 00:05:02,580
moved in under a minute.

140
00:05:02,760 --> 00:05:06,668
Finnish icebreaker designers were among the pioneers

141
00:05:06,668 --> 00:05:07,570
of the idea,

142
00:05:07,570 --> 00:05:10,877
because the Baltic Sea freezes every winter,

143
00:05:10,877 --> 00:05:13,507
and keeping its ports open is vital

144
00:05:13,507 --> 00:05:15,311
for the country's trade.

145
00:05:15,661 --> 00:05:18,559
Why do some icebreakers blow air bubbles

146
00:05:18,559 --> 00:05:19,863
out of their hull?

147
00:05:20,043 --> 00:05:21,483
To reduce friction.

148
00:05:21,663 --> 00:05:24,500
Holes along the lower hull release a

149
00:05:24,500 --> 00:05:26,470
stream of compressed air.

150
00:05:26,650 --> 00:05:29,229
The bubbles rise along the sides of

151
00:05:29,229 --> 00:05:31,808
the ship, carrying water with them,

152
00:05:31,808 --> 00:05:34,534
and form a slippery layer between the

153
00:05:34,534 --> 00:05:35,787
hull and the ice.

154
00:05:36,137 --> 00:05:39,161
Ice and snow piled against a hull

155
00:05:39,161 --> 00:05:40,811
can act like glue,

156
00:05:40,811 --> 00:05:43,927
especially wet snow on top of ice.

157
00:05:44,107 --> 00:05:46,927
The bubbling water washes it away and

158
00:05:46,927 --> 00:05:49,366
makes the ice slide more easily.

159
00:05:49,546 --> 00:05:52,289
The ship needs less power to move,

160
00:05:52,289 --> 00:05:54,790
and is less likely to get stuck

161
00:05:54,790 --> 00:05:55,839
in heavy ice.

162
00:05:56,189 --> 00:05:59,140
Some icebreakers use a very smooth,

163
00:05:59,140 --> 00:06:01,922
hard coating on the hull instead,

164
00:06:01,922 --> 00:06:05,632
which also reduces friction and protects the

165
00:06:05,632 --> 00:06:06,138
steel.

166
00:06:06,318 --> 00:06:09,151
Both ideas aim at the same problem.

167
00:06:09,331 --> 00:06:11,391
Breaking the ice is only half the

168
00:06:11,391 --> 00:06:11,827
battle.

169
00:06:12,007 --> 00:06:14,098
The other half is sliding through the

170
00:06:14,098 --> 00:06:16,245
broken pieces without being held back.

171
00:06:16,595 --> 00:06:19,595
Why do most icebreakers turn their propellers

172
00:06:19,595 --> 00:06:20,995
with electric motors?

173
00:06:21,175 --> 00:06:24,077
Because the propellers keep hitting ice.

174
00:06:24,257 --> 00:06:27,032
A block of ice can suddenly jam

175
00:06:27,032 --> 00:06:29,807
a propeller and almost stop it.

176
00:06:29,987 --> 00:06:32,906
A diesel engine connected directly to the

177
00:06:32,906 --> 00:06:35,612
propeller could stall or be damaged by

178
00:06:35,612 --> 00:06:36,396
that shock.

179
00:06:36,746 --> 00:06:39,258
In a diesel electric icebreaker,

180
00:06:39,258 --> 00:06:41,928
the diesel engines run generators,

181
00:06:41,928 --> 00:06:45,462
and the generators power electric motors that

182
00:06:45,462 --> 00:06:47,032
turn the propellers.

183
00:06:47,212 --> 00:06:50,540
An electric motor can produce full force

184
00:06:50,540 --> 00:06:52,454
even at very low speed,

185
00:06:52,454 --> 00:06:55,117
so when ice slows the propeller,

186
00:06:55,117 --> 00:06:58,278
the motor keeps pushing hard and chews

187
00:06:58,278 --> 00:06:59,194
through it.

188
00:06:59,544 --> 00:07:02,667
The propellers themselves are made of very

189
00:07:02,667 --> 00:07:05,046
strong steel, with thick blades,

190
00:07:05,046 --> 00:07:07,425
and they are often designed so a

191
00:07:07,425 --> 00:07:09,656
damaged blade can be replaced.

192
00:07:09,836 --> 00:07:13,054
Some icebreakers have propellers at the bow

193
00:07:13,054 --> 00:07:15,525
too, to wash broken ice away from

194
00:07:15,525 --> 00:07:16,198
the hull.

195
00:07:16,378 --> 00:07:19,010
Every part of the drive is built

196
00:07:19,010 --> 00:07:20,244
to survive ice.

197
00:07:20,595 --> 00:07:24,291
Why does Russia operate icebreakers powered by

198
00:07:24,291 --> 00:07:25,657
nuclear reactors?

199
00:07:25,837 --> 00:07:28,414
Because of distance and power.

200
00:07:28,594 --> 00:07:31,289
The Arctic route along the north of

201
00:07:31,289 --> 00:07:32,829
Russia is very long,

202
00:07:32,829 --> 00:07:35,369
far from ports where a ship could

203
00:07:35,369 --> 00:07:35,908
refuel.

204
00:07:36,088 --> 00:07:39,748
Breaking thick ice also needs enormous power

205
00:07:39,748 --> 00:07:41,162
for long periods.

206
00:07:41,512 --> 00:07:45,026
A nuclear reactor provides that power without

207
00:07:45,026 --> 00:07:46,666
refuelling for years.

208
00:07:46,846 --> 00:07:49,693
The Soviet Union launched the Lenin in

209
00:07:49,693 --> 00:07:53,814
nineteen fifty-seven, the world's first nuclear powered

210
00:07:53,814 --> 00:07:54,788
surface ship.

211
00:07:54,968 --> 00:07:57,048
In nineteen seventy-seven,

212
00:07:57,048 --> 00:08:00,808
the nuclear icebreaker Arktika became the first

213
00:08:00,808 --> 00:08:03,768
surface ship to reach the North Pole.

214
00:08:04,118 --> 00:08:07,617
Russia still builds new nuclear icebreakers,

215
00:08:07,617 --> 00:08:10,322
among the most powerful ever made,

216
00:08:10,322 --> 00:08:13,185
to keep the Northern Sea Route open.

217
00:08:13,365 --> 00:08:16,674
Nuclear power is expensive and complex,

218
00:08:16,674 --> 00:08:19,814
and only makes sense for the toughest

219
00:08:19,814 --> 00:08:20,238
jobs.

220
00:08:20,418 --> 00:08:21,462
In the Arctic,

221
00:08:21,462 --> 00:08:23,997
where the ice never stops and fuel

222
00:08:23,997 --> 00:08:24,892
is far away,

223
00:08:24,892 --> 00:08:27,129
it is the toughest job of all.

224
00:08:27,479 --> 00:08:30,577
Why do some ships sail backwards through

225
00:08:30,577 --> 00:08:31,739
ice on purpose?

226
00:08:31,919 --> 00:08:34,381
They are called double acting ships.

227
00:08:34,561 --> 00:08:37,221
The bow is shaped for open water,

228
00:08:37,221 --> 00:08:39,963
so the ship is efficient in normal

229
00:08:39,963 --> 00:08:40,366
seas.

230
00:08:40,546 --> 00:08:43,465
The stern is shaped like an icebreaker,

231
00:08:43,465 --> 00:08:46,385
and it has rotating propeller pods that

232
00:08:46,385 --> 00:08:48,406
can break and mill the ice.

233
00:08:48,756 --> 00:08:49,831
In heavy ice,

234
00:08:49,831 --> 00:08:52,810
the ship turns around and goes stern

235
00:08:52,810 --> 00:08:53,307
first.

236
00:08:53,487 --> 00:08:56,019
The propellers chew up the ice in

237
00:08:56,019 --> 00:08:58,321
front of them and wash it away

238
00:08:58,321 --> 00:08:59,472
along the hull,

239
00:08:59,472 --> 00:09:02,312
which turns out to be very effective,

240
00:09:02,312 --> 00:09:03,846
especially in thick,

241
00:09:03,846 --> 00:09:04,690
packed ice.

242
00:09:04,870 --> 00:09:06,108
In open water,

243
00:09:06,108 --> 00:09:09,380
the ship turns around again and sails

244
00:09:09,380 --> 00:09:10,176
normally.

245
00:09:10,526 --> 00:09:13,430
This idea was developed in Finland,

246
00:09:13,430 --> 00:09:16,334
and it allowed some cargo ships and

247
00:09:16,334 --> 00:09:19,487
tankers to sail in ice without needing

248
00:09:19,487 --> 00:09:21,230
an icebreaker escort.

249
00:09:21,410 --> 00:09:24,311
It is a perfect example of engineering

250
00:09:24,311 --> 00:09:24,998
thinking.

251
00:09:25,178 --> 00:09:27,439
When one end of the ship cannot

252
00:09:27,439 --> 00:09:28,898
be good at two jobs,

253
00:09:28,898 --> 00:09:31,087
give each end a different job.

254
00:09:31,437 --> 00:09:33,931
Why do some icebreakers have a curved

255
00:09:33,931 --> 00:09:35,953
notch cut into the back of the

256
00:09:35,953 --> 00:09:36,290
hull?

257
00:09:36,470 --> 00:09:38,060
It is a towing notch.

258
00:09:38,240 --> 00:09:40,749
When a cargo ship gets stuck in

259
00:09:40,749 --> 00:09:43,743
heavy ice, the icebreaker can pull it

260
00:09:43,743 --> 00:09:46,818
very close, until the cargo ship's bow

261
00:09:46,818 --> 00:09:49,084
sits right inside the notch.

262
00:09:49,434 --> 00:09:51,768
Then the two ships move together as

263
00:09:51,768 --> 00:09:52,034
one.

264
00:09:52,214 --> 00:09:54,293
The icebreaker breaks the ice,

265
00:09:54,293 --> 00:09:56,925
and the cargo ship pushes from behind,

266
00:09:56,925 --> 00:09:59,073
with no gap where the ice could

267
00:09:59,073 --> 00:10:00,597
close in between them.

268
00:10:00,777 --> 00:10:01,802
In thick ice,

269
00:10:01,802 --> 00:10:04,484
a channel can freeze shut quickly,

270
00:10:04,484 --> 00:10:07,639
so keeping the ships tightly together is

271
00:10:07,639 --> 00:10:08,428
essential.

272
00:10:08,778 --> 00:10:11,515
The notch is padded with heavy rubber

273
00:10:11,515 --> 00:10:13,735
fenders to protect both ships.

274
00:10:13,915 --> 00:10:16,374
It is a simple solution that looks

275
00:10:16,374 --> 00:10:17,749
strange in a photo,

276
00:10:17,749 --> 00:10:19,774
like a bite taken out of the

277
00:10:19,774 --> 00:10:20,208
stern.

278
00:10:20,388 --> 00:10:21,572
But in the Arctic,

279
00:10:21,572 --> 00:10:23,809
it can be the difference between a

280
00:10:23,809 --> 00:10:26,506
cargo ship reaching port and spending the

281
00:10:26,506 --> 00:10:27,493
winter trapped.

282
00:10:27,843 --> 00:10:31,618
Why does one icebreaker sail sideways through

283
00:10:31,618 --> 00:10:32,289
the ice?

284
00:10:32,469 --> 00:10:33,987
It is the Baltika,

285
00:10:33,987 --> 00:10:37,276
an oblique icebreaker built for Russia.

286
